1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,571
This video practises program understanding on five short Java programs from the question bank.

2
00:00:07,021 --> 00:00:20,375
First, four versions of linear search. We draw an object diagram for each one at the moment it finds a match. Then a small class hierarchy, where we find the target of each polymorphic call.

3
00:00:20,825 --> 00:00:24,708
The slides list what we know about a program when we understand it.

4
00:00:25,158 --> 00:00:27,569
We know its inputs: what goes in.

5
00:00:28,019 --> 00:00:31,432
We know its outputs. Outputs include the errors.

6
00:00:31,882 --> 00:00:36,554
We know its static structure: the data structures and the class diagram.

7
00:00:37,004 --> 00:00:45,153
We know its dynamic execution: the call graph, pointers and aliasing, and object diagrams at points in time.

8
00:00:45,603 --> 00:00:50,510
And we know its algorithms: runtime, space, and when the program ends.

9
00:00:50,960 --> 00:01:01,029
The four linear search programs have the same inputs, outputs and algorithm. They differ only in static structure and in dynamic execution.

10
00:01:01,479 --> 00:01:07,815
Here are the four programs of questions-pre section 2.2. They solve the same problem.

11
00:01:08,265 --> 00:01:17,887
The input is a list of strings on the command line. The first string is the token to search for. The program searches for it in the rest of the list.

12
00:01:18,337 --> 00:01:25,057
The output is found it, or didn't find it. If no list is given, the program fails with an error.

13
00:01:25,507 --> 00:01:31,565
The four programs use two data structures: an array, or a linked list of Node objects.

14
00:01:32,015 --> 00:01:39,461
And they use two control structures: iteration, which is a loop, or recursion, a method that calls itself.

15
00:01:39,911 --> 00:01:52,839
The algorithm is the same in all four: linear search. The runtime is order n, the space is order n, and the program ends when it finds the token or reaches the end of the list.

16
00:01:53,289 --> 00:02:00,286
Each solution is an object diagram. This is the notation from the course notes, section 2.2.

17
00:02:00,736 --> 00:02:05,877
The PC tag gives the program counter: the line that is about to execute.

18
00:02:06,327 --> 00:02:13,815
A stack frame has one slot for each local variable, parameters included. A method call adds a frame.

19
00:02:14,265 --> 00:02:22,543
An object in the heap is an ellipse. Its name is the class name plus a number that you choose. Only new makes an object.

20
00:02:22,993 --> 00:02:32,123
A solid arrow is a field of one object that refers to another object. The label is the field name or the array index.

21
00:02:32,573 --> 00:02:44,925
A dotted arrow is a variable on the stack that refers to an object. A null variable has no arrow. Arrows go from the stack or the heap into the heap, never from stack to stack.

22
00:02:45,375 --> 00:02:59,541
Question 2.2.1. This is the array and loop version. The input on the command line is Moe, Larry, Curly, Moe. Draw the object diagram at the moment the program finds the match.

23
00:02:59,991 --> 00:03:01,633
Pause the video and try it.

24
00:03:02,083 --> 00:03:08,867
Before line 3 runs, the runtime made the args array from the command line. args refers to it.

25
00:03:09,317 --> 00:03:14,117
The array has four slots. Slot 0 and slot 3 refer to Moe.

26
00:03:14,567 --> 00:03:18,621
Slot 1 refers to Larry, and slot 2 refers to Curly.

27
00:03:19,071 --> 00:03:27,348
Line 3. toFind gets the object at index 0, so toFind refers to Moe. No new object is made.

28
00:03:27,798 --> 00:03:35,201
Line 4. The loop starts with i equal to 1. Line 5 compares Moe with Larry. They are not equal.

29
00:03:35,651 --> 00:03:39,043
i becomes 2. Moe is not equal to Curly.

30
00:03:39,493 --> 00:03:42,181
i becomes 3. Moe equals Moe.

31
00:03:42,631 --> 00:03:50,823
The program is about to run line 6 and print found it. So the PC is 6. This is the diagram for the solution.

32
00:03:51,273 --> 00:03:55,390
There is one frame, main. The slot i changes in place.

33
00:03:55,840 --> 00:04:05,099
The posted solution draws one Moe object for index 0 and index 3. equals compares the letters, so the result is the same.

34
00:04:05,549 --> 00:04:15,959
Question 2.2.2. The same array, but search calls itself. Same input. Draw the object diagram when the program finds the match.

35
00:04:16,409 --> 00:04:18,052
Pause the video and try it.

36
00:04:18,502 --> 00:04:26,395
Line 3 runs as before. main has two slots: args refers to the array, and toFind refers to Moe.

37
00:04:26,845 --> 00:04:36,040
Line 4 calls search with i equal to 1. A call makes a new frame with one slot per parameter: toFind, a, and i.

38
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:42,357
toFind and a are copies of references, so they refer to the same objects as in main.

39
00:04:42,807 --> 00:04:47,329
Line 9 compares Moe with a of 1, which is Larry. Not equal.

40
00:04:47,779 --> 00:04:59,491
Line 13. i is 1, which is less than a dot length minus 1, which is 3. So search calls itself with i equal to 2. A second search frame appears.

41
00:04:59,941 --> 00:05:02,331
Line 9: Moe is not Curly.

42
00:05:02,781 --> 00:05:10,290
Line 13: 2 is less than 3, so search calls itself with i equal to 3. A third frame appears.

43
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:15,796
Line 9: Moe equals Moe. The next line is 10, so the PC is 10.

44
00:05:16,246 --> 00:05:23,094
The solution has four frames: main and three search frames. Each search frame has its own i.

45
00:05:23,544 --> 00:05:27,981
The old frames stay on the stack, because no call has returned yet.

46
00:05:28,431 --> 00:05:37,861
Compare the two array programs. In the loop version, the call graph is only main. One frame holds i, and i changes in place.

47
00:05:38,311 --> 00:05:45,329
In the recursive version, main calls search, and search calls itself. The call graph has a loop on search.

48
00:05:45,779 --> 00:05:54,889
At the match, the loop version has one frame. The recursive version has main plus one search frame per element it visited, here three.

49
00:05:55,339 --> 00:06:03,296
In the worst case the token is not there, and the recursion makes one frame per element. That is order n stack space.

50
00:06:03,746 --> 00:06:17,783
Question 2.2.3. Now the program first copies the array into a linked list of Node objects. Then it searches the list with a loop. Same input. Draw the object diagram at the match.

51
00:06:18,233 --> 00:06:19,876
Pause the video and try it.

52
00:06:20,326 --> 00:06:27,174
First, the Node class. Both fields are final, so the constructor sets them once, and they never change.

53
00:06:27,624 --> 00:06:34,387
In main, line 3 runs as before: args refers to the array, and toFind refers to Moe.

54
00:06:34,837 --> 00:06:39,146
Line 5 sets head to null. A null variable has no arrow.

55
00:06:39,596 --> 00:06:49,516
The loop starts at the end of the array, with i equal to 3. new makes Node 1. Its next is the old head, null. Its data refers to Moe.

56
00:06:49,966 --> 00:06:51,950
Then head refers to Node 1.

57
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:58,651
i equal to 2. new makes Node 2. Its next is Node 1, and its data is Curly.

58
00:06:59,101 --> 00:07:03,303
The old head arrow is erased. head now refers to Node 2.

59
00:07:03,753 --> 00:07:12,436
i equal to 1. Node 3 gets next equal to Node 2, and data equal to Larry. head now refers to Node 3.

60
00:07:12,886 --> 00:07:18,326
i equal to 0. The test i greater than 0 is false, so the loop ends.

61
00:07:18,776 --> 00:07:23,149
The list reads Larry, Curly, Moe, in the same order as the array.

62
00:07:23,599 --> 00:07:30,895
The program builds from the back because the next field is final. A node needs its next node to exist first.

63
00:07:31,345 --> 00:07:36,487
Now the search. Line 10 sets n to head, so n refers to Node 3.

64
00:07:36,937 --> 00:07:41,950
Line 12 compares Moe with n dot data, which is Larry. Not equal.

65
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:49,653
Line 16 sets n to n dot next. The arrow from n is erased and drawn again, to Node 2.

66
00:07:50,103 --> 00:07:54,327
Line 12: the data of Node 2 is Curly. Not equal.

67
00:07:54,777 --> 00:07:57,785
Line 16 moves n again, to Node 1.

68
00:07:58,235 --> 00:08:01,969
Line 12: the data of Node 1 is Moe. Equal.

69
00:08:02,419 --> 00:08:05,939
The next line is 13, so the PC is 13.

70
00:08:06,389 --> 00:08:09,759
There is one frame. Only the arrow from n moved.

71
00:08:10,209 --> 00:08:19,020
The slot i belongs to the first loop, which is over. The posted solution keeps an empty i slot. You can also leave it out.

72
00:08:19,470 --> 00:08:31,331
Question 2.2.4. The last variant. It builds the same linked list, then search calls itself on n dot next. Draw the object diagram at the match.

73
00:08:31,781 --> 00:08:33,424
Pause the video and try it.

74
00:08:33,874 --> 00:08:42,002
The list is built in the same way as before. The posted solution shows i equal to 0, the last value of the loop.

75
00:08:42,452 --> 00:08:49,855
Line 10 calls search with head. The new frame has two slots, toFind and n. n refers to Node 3.

76
00:08:50,305 --> 00:08:55,339
Line 18 compares Moe with the data of Node 3, Larry. Not equal.

77
00:08:55,789 --> 00:09:02,488
Line 22 calls search with n dot next. A second frame. Its n refers to Node 2.

78
00:09:02,938 --> 00:09:05,498
Line 18: Curly is not Moe.

79
00:09:05,948 --> 00:09:11,729
Line 22 calls search again. A third frame. Its n refers to Node 1.

80
00:09:12,179 --> 00:09:18,174
Line 18: Moe equals Moe. The next line is 19, so the PC is 19.

81
00:09:18,624 --> 00:09:23,189
The stack has main and three search frames. Each frame has its own n.

82
00:09:23,639 --> 00:09:28,461
Line 16, the return when n is null, does not run for this input.

83
00:09:28,911 --> 00:09:36,079
Here are the four solutions side by side. The PC at the match is 6, 10, 13 and 19.

84
00:09:36,529 --> 00:09:42,694
The recursive versions have main plus three search frames. The iterative versions have only main.

85
00:09:43,144 --> 00:09:49,779
The linked list versions add three Node objects to the heap. new runs three times, once per node.

86
00:09:50,229 --> 00:09:57,994
In each program, something different moves: the value of i, a new frame, the arrow from n, or a new frame per node.

87
00:09:58,444 --> 00:10:10,156
All four use order n space. The constant factors differ: nodes and frames add more memory. This is why the slides list both big O and constant factors.

88
00:10:10,606 --> 00:10:24,686
Program understanding includes the errors. We ran the four programs on short inputs. This check is ours, not from the posted solution. With Moe, Larry, Curly, Moe, all four print found it.

89
00:10:25,136 --> 00:10:32,795
With Moe, Larry, Curly, the token is not in the rest of the list. All four print didn't find it and exit with 1.

90
00:10:33,245 --> 00:10:51,613
With only Moe, three programs print didn't find it. Array Recursive fails. Line 4 calls search with i equal to 1 without a test. Line 9 reads a of 1, but the array has only one slot. Java throws an array index out of bounds exception.

91
00:10:52,063 --> 00:11:01,513
With no input, all four fail at line 3, because args of 0 does not exist. This is the error that the question bank lists.

92
00:11:01,963 --> 00:11:13,249
Now the second topic. Course notes section 2.1 gives the terms. B is the base class, or super class. It has a field x and a method foo.

93
00:11:13,699 --> 00:11:27,416
D extends B. D is the derived class, or sub class. D defines foo again. The definition in D overrides the one in B. In C plus plus terms, foo is a virtual method.

94
00:11:27,866 --> 00:11:32,325
Here is a call site, r dot foo. Two methods named foo exist.

95
00:11:32,775 --> 00:11:37,767
If r refers to an object of class B, the call runs B dot foo.

96
00:11:38,217 --> 00:11:43,678
If r refers to an object of class D, the same call runs D dot foo.

97
00:11:44,128 --> 00:11:48,565
The class of the object decides the target, when the program runs.

98
00:11:49,015 --> 00:11:57,463
A call site with more than one possible target is polymorphic. A call site with only one target is monomorphic.

99
00:11:57,913 --> 00:12:18,116
Question 2.3.1. This is a simple version of the A S T code for the language F. Draw the class diagram, and the object diagram when the PC is 29 and i is 2. Give the input, the output, and the targets of the polymorphic call on line 28.

100
00:12:18,566 --> 00:12:20,209
Pause the video and try it.

101
00:12:20,659 --> 00:12:34,931
Start the class diagram at the top, with lines 1 and 2. Expr is abstract. It declares one abstract method, operator. An italic name means abstract: no object of class Expr can exist.

102
00:12:35,381 --> 00:12:51,807
Lines 4 to 8. BinaryExpr extends Expr. The hollow triangle means extends. It has two final fields, left and right, of type Expr. It declares the abstract method newBinaryExpr, and it defines toString.

103
00:12:52,257 --> 00:13:09,409
Lines 10 to 16. AndExpr and OrExpr extend BinaryExpr. Each one defines operator and newBinaryExpr. They are the only concrete classes, so every object in this program is an AndExpr or an OrExpr.

104
00:13:09,859 --> 00:13:18,606
One detail for later. toString is defined only in BinaryExpr, on line 8. AndExpr and OrExpr inherit it.

105
00:13:19,056 --> 00:13:24,773
Now run main. Line 20. new makes AndExpr 1, and b refers to it.

106
00:13:25,223 --> 00:13:38,983
The declared type of b is BinaryExpr, but the class of the object is AndExpr. b can refer to an object of any subclass of BinaryExpr. This answers the comment on line 20.

107
00:13:39,433 --> 00:13:46,580
Line 21 makes an array with three slots. They are all null, so array 1 has no arrows yet.

108
00:13:47,030 --> 00:13:57,697
Line 23. a of 0 gets b. There is no new, so no new object. Slot 0 and b refer to the same object. They are aliases.

109
00:13:58,147 --> 00:14:02,392
Line 24. new makes OrExpr 1, in slot 1.

110
00:14:02,842 --> 00:14:13,658
Line 25 calls newBinaryExpr on b. b refers to an AndExpr, so line 12 runs, and new makes AndExpr 2 in slot 2.

111
00:14:14,108 --> 00:14:21,852
So the objects in the array are AndExpr, OrExpr, AndExpr. That answers the comment on line 22.

112
00:14:22,302 --> 00:14:28,531
The loop. With i equal to 0, line 27 makes e refer to AndExpr 1.

113
00:14:28,981 --> 00:14:34,613
With i equal to 1, the arrow from e is erased and drawn to OrExpr 1.

114
00:14:35,063 --> 00:14:38,839
With i equal to 2, e refers to AndExpr 2.

115
00:14:39,289 --> 00:14:43,641
The next line is 29. This is the requested diagram.

116
00:14:44,091 --> 00:14:48,358
The frame of main has args, b, a, i equal to 2, and e.

117
00:14:48,808 --> 00:14:57,235
First line 25, which the code calls monomorphic. b is assigned once, on line 20, to AndExpr 1.

118
00:14:57,685 --> 00:15:02,719
So the call always runs AndExpr dot newBinaryExpr. One target.

119
00:15:03,169 --> 00:15:13,751
Line 28 calls operator on e. With i equal to 0, e refers to AndExpr 1. The target is AndExpr dot operator.

120
00:15:14,201 --> 00:15:22,137
With i equal to 1, e refers to OrExpr 1. The same line now runs OrExpr dot operator.

121
00:15:22,587 --> 00:15:33,189
With i equal to 2, e refers to AndExpr 2. The target is AndExpr dot operator again. This is the table in the posted solution.

122
00:15:33,639 --> 00:15:41,853
One call site, two targets, chosen by the class of the object. So line 28 is a polymorphic call site.

123
00:15:42,303 --> 00:15:50,815
The code asks if line 29 is mono or polymorphic. The posted solution does not answer. Here is our solution.

124
00:15:51,265 --> 00:15:57,985
toString is defined only in BinaryExpr. AndExpr and OrExpr do not override it.

125
00:15:58,435 --> 00:16:04,451
So for all three objects, the call runs BinaryExpr dot toString. One target.

126
00:16:04,901 --> 00:16:13,199
But inside toString, the call to operator is polymorphic. That call picks and or or, by the class of the object.

127
00:16:13,649 --> 00:16:17,361
So line 29 is monomorphic in this program.

128
00:16:17,811 --> 00:16:29,566
Java still compiles it as a virtual call. If a new subclass overrides toString, line 29 becomes polymorphic. We checked the targets by running the code.

129
00:16:30,016 --> 00:16:33,173
The input is none. main never reads args.

130
00:16:33,623 --> 00:16:39,021
With i equal to 0, line 28 prints and, with a space on each side.

131
00:16:39,471 --> 00:16:52,036
Line 29 prints null and null. toString joins left, operator, and right. Both fields of AndExpr 1 are null, and joining null with a string gives the word null.

132
00:16:52,486 --> 00:16:56,411
With i equal to 1, the lines are or, then null or null.

133
00:16:56,861 --> 00:17:02,237
With i equal to 2, and, then null and null again. Six lines in total.

134
00:17:02,687 --> 00:17:09,706
The posted solution writes and without spaces. The method returns and with one space on each side.

135
00:17:10,156 --> 00:17:16,108
This slide is optional. It is from course notes section 5.4, later in the course.

136
00:17:16,558 --> 00:17:22,873
Compiled code must find the target of a call. Each object has a header that names its class.

137
00:17:23,323 --> 00:17:34,565
In one translation, the header points to a type information block, tib, with a vtable. A vtable is an array of function pointers, one slot per method.

138
00:17:35,015 --> 00:17:42,162
A polymorphic call loads the slot for operator from the vtable of the object's class, then jumps there.

139
00:17:42,612 --> 00:17:48,351
OrExpr has its own vtable, with OrExpr dot operator in slot 0.

140
00:17:48,801 --> 00:17:57,227
The notes also show a second translation, a switch on a type number. You do not need this for question 2.3.1.

141
00:17:57,677 --> 00:18:08,365
To summarize. An object diagram shows the PC, the frames with one slot per variable, the objects, and the arrows. Only new makes an object.

142
00:18:08,815 --> 00:18:17,242
A loop keeps one frame and changes values in place. Recursion adds one frame per call, each with its own parameters.

143
00:18:17,692 --> 00:18:22,833
An immutable linked list is built from the back, because the next field is final.

144
00:18:23,283 --> 00:18:29,342
Outputs include errors. Test the short inputs: no arguments, and one argument.

145
00:18:29,792 --> 00:18:38,091
The declared type of a variable is not the class of the object. At a virtual call, the class of the object picks the target.

146
00:18:38,541 --> 00:18:51,789
A call site with more than one target is polymorphic. A call site with exactly one target is monomorphic. Next video: complexity, undecidability and programming paradigms.
